From c8863853b104431ffc83821901bcdfc687c7f84a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 09:40:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] kanban: missing-parent repair grant refuses a
 re-held child and a stale repair run

Review follow-ups for PR #1945 (reviews 5888194553 and 5887767012):

- A hold placed after the parent_missing block no longer reads as that block.
  missing_parent_repair_grant and the operator call's own-actor check
  (_agent_block) used the newest `blocked` event only. The failure breaker
  writes `gave_up`, not `blocked`, so a child that was released and then
  re-held by the breaker still looked parent_missing (or own-agent) blocked,
  and the repair card or the operator call could release a policy hold.
  New kanban_requeue.current_block_payload returns {} when an `unblocked` or
  `gave_up` event is newer than the newest `blocked`; both checks use it.
- The kanban_unblock tool path now applies the owner-wake path's run check
  inside the unblock txn: the repair card must be `running` with
  current_run_id == _worker_run_id(env_tid), so a reclaimed earlier run
  cannot act for the live one.
- Operator call: the repair_task_id/via annotation is decided inside the
  unblock_task precondition, so it credits the repair grant only when that
  grant is what passed in the txn.
- Tests: breaker-hold repro (tool path, 3 event orders; operator path, after a
  release and after an own-agent block), human_request refusal on both paths,
  stale-run refusal (not running / reclaimed / unbound), plus refusals for a
  block naming this card on another child, a card the detector did not file,
  and a later non-detector block naming the repair card.
